Nick and Josh,
&nbsp;
Following our call today, we would like to update you further on PG’s discussion with Toremis about the ATE top-ups for emissions and initiate the approval process required for compliance with the Credit
 Agreements of 2 October 2023, 23 August 2024, and 14 August 2025 (“Credit Agreements”).
&nbsp;
Structure Overview
&nbsp;
Gramercy Guarantee
&nbsp;
We confirmed that obtaining a guarantee from Gramercy would be challenging, and Toremis appeared comfortable proceeding without it, provided that the structure outlined below is acceptable.
&nbsp;
Cross-Collateralisation

This is a potential dealbreaker for at least some of the cases requiring top-ups. Toremis is seeking a mechanism akin to the existing overarching deed (“Wrapper”) but limited to cross-collateralisation among the Nox cases only.
&nbsp;
Because the risk profile varies across the Nox cases, they have proposed a separate cross-collateralisation agreement that would apply to the eight top-up policies. Toremis is aware of the existing cross-collateralisation
 provisions within the Wrapper and is comfortable that the new arrangement would rank below these, as altering the current priority would require changes to the existing structure.
&nbsp;
We will need to discuss further how the cross-collateralisation of the additional layers would coexist within the waterfall, particularly in relation to the cross-collateralisation of emissions proceeds in
 the 4th layer of the BHP ATE policy (as formalised in the Reimbursement Deed), which sets out payment of reimbursement out of 50% of PG net proceeds from Nox cases after taxes, insurers, counsel, and payments to funders, including Gramercy facilities.
&nbsp;
Economic Terms of the Top-Up
&nbsp;
The upfront premium, D&amp;C, reimbursement of adverse costs, and other terms and conditions would mirror those of the existing layers of the ATE policies and the individual Priorities Deeds. This additional
 layer would sit above the current layers with respect to reimbursements and D&amp;C premiums payable within each Nox case.
&nbsp;
As discussed, to cover insurers’ risk arising from the AAE to be given to defendants, they would require PG to provide an indemnity for any potential claims paid by the insurers that fall within the exclusion
 circumstances set out in the ATE policies — effectively operating as a clawback mechanism. This indemnity would also require approval from the Gramercy lenders under the Credit Agreements.
&nbsp;
Next Steps
&nbsp;
The next step is for PG to share a simplified model illustrating how the case proceeds would flow and how the cross-collateralisation would operate. It was unclear from the call whether Toremis is fully across
 the details of the mechanics of the overarching Priorities Deed currently in place. Toremis expects to provide us with a quote by the end of next week, and we will need to be prepared to move quickly to incept the policies.
&nbsp;
Approvals
&nbsp;
In light of the above, we would like to start the process for approval by the Gramercy lenders to ensure compliance with Section 6.21 regarding the amounts defined as “Third Party Fees” (described in Schedule
 III). We understand that Gramercy may also need to seek consent from the CPI insurers. Could you please confirm how long that process is expected to take? Please let us know if there is anything else we can do to facilitate or expedite the necessary approvals
 at this stage.
&nbsp;
Kind regards,
&nbsp;
PG
