oodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;30 September 2025 10:25To:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;jonathan.simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation&nbsp;Dear James and Jonathan,I hope you're well.I am writing regarding the Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation, specifically regarding the viability of securing additional LOI coverage through further excess layer insurance policies.Following the handing down of the judgment (10th September 2025) after the Second Costs Management Hearing (15-16 July 2025), the Defendants have revisited their position on applications for security for costs. We have therefore undertaken a further review of the potential adverse costs resulting from the Tranche 3 Quantum Trial and the Second General Budget.&nbsp;Previously, our assessment of ATE sufficiency was calculated on the following basis:60% of the Defendants’ claimed incurred costs;60% of the Defendants’ claimed budgeted costs; and100% of approved future costs to Spring 2026.Given the level of incurred costs&nbsp;and the criticisms between Defendants regarding their figures, we consider that the sufficiency calculation can be revised downwards without materially affecting the adequacy of coverage. We propose the following revised basis:50% of the Defendants’ claimed incurred costs;50% of the Defendants’ claimed budgeted costs; and100% of approved future costs to 31 July 2026.On this revised basis, the following GLOs fall below the proposed coverage threshold:&nbsp;Mercedes&nbsp;-£953,580.35Ford-£838,961.27Renault-Nissan-£2,535,219.93PCD-£149,635.79Toyota-£136,927.40&nbsp;We would be grateful for your confirmation on whether additional excess layer coverage can be secured for these cases, specifically for&nbsp;Mercedes, Ford, and Renault-Nissan.We look forw