 discuss further how the cross-collateralisation of the additional layers would coexist within the waterfall, particularly in relation to the cross-collateralisation of emissions proceeds in the 4th layer of the BHP ATE policy (as formalised in the Reimbursement Deed), which sets out payment of reimbursement out of 50% of PG net proceeds from Nox cases after taxes, insurers, counsel, and payments to funders, including Gramercy facilities.&nbsp;Economic Terms of the Top-Up&nbsp;The upfront premium, D&amp;C, reimbursement of adverse costs, and other terms and conditions would mirror those of the existing layers of the ATE policies and the individual Priorities Deeds. This additional layer would sit above the current layers with respect to reimbursements and D&amp;C premiums payable within each Nox case.&nbsp;As discussed, to cover insurers’ risk arising from the AAE to be given to defendants, they would require PG to provide an indemnity for any potential claims paid by the insurers that fall within the exclusion circumstances set out in the ATE policies — effectively operating as a clawback mechanism. This indemnity would also require approval from the Gramercy lenders under the Credit Agreements.&nbsp;Next Steps&nbsp;The next step is for PG to share a simplified model illustrating how the case proceeds would flow and how the cross-collateralisation would operate. It was unclear from the call whether Toremis is fully across the details of the mechanics of the overarching Priorities Deed currently in place. Toremis expects to provide us with a quote by the end of next week, and we will need to be prepared to move quickly to incept the policies.&nbsp;Approvals&nbsp;In light of the above, we would like to start the process for approval by the Gramercy lenders to ensure compliance with Section 6.21 regarding the amounts defined as “Third Party Fees” (described in Schedule III). We understand that Gramercy may also need to seek consent from the CPI insurers. Could you please confirm how long that process is expected to take? Please let us know if there is anything else we can do to facilitate or expedite the necessary approvals at this stage.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;PG&nbsp;​​​​Carolina&nbsp;Koschdoski&nbsp;de&nbsp;SouzaLegal&nbsp;DirectorFrom: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 06 November 2025 15:43To: Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt;Cc: Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;Hi Nick &nbsp;We have received some further queries from our brokers regarding the proposed topup to the Nox policies (+£17m LOI)&nbsp;They want a call tomorrow to discuss 1b, would it be possible to discuss with you on a quick call before hand?&nbsp;Query from insurer below, let me know if you are available &nbsp;TomOn the economics - we will review the existing cross-collateralisation arrangements with a view to how they might be adapted for our purposes. We do have two requests on this side of things though:We note that for the existing arrangements, PG is the only claimant-side party. Our current view is that we would also want Gramercy as a party, either in its own right or as a guarantor. Is this acceptable to PG/Gramercy?On a related point, we would like to better understand how any settlement proceeds would flow through the existing waterfall(s), including the arrangements with cash flow up to Gramercy. Essentially, we would like to be able to understand what the effect of, for example, a settlement with Mercedes, would have on the money flowing through the waterfall in order to assess the extent to which such a settlement would then protect us from adverse results against other defendants. It would also be useful to be able to see the effect of varying levels of settlement. We assume that PG has excel 